          "reasoning": "The alerted systemctl process (proc-a4e223555e, 'systemctl stop apache2' running as root) was started by 'sudo systemctl stop apache2', which user ubuntu typed into their own interactive login shell ('-bash', proc-4ab4cb0f23). Read in order, that shell's commands look like an admin installing and testing a web server: sudo apt update, sudo apt install apache2, service --status-all, a bare 'shred' with no arguments (it exits after 4 ms and touches no file), sudo systemctl disable apache2, sudo systemctl stop apache2, sudo systemctl status apache2, then sudo systemctl restart apache2. apache2 had been installed minutes earlier in the same session, and the session ends by restarting it, so nothing points to deliberately denying service. The only child process was systemd-tty-ask-password-agent, which systemctl starts normally. The only network activity was a local DNS lookup to 127.0.0.53. I found no script, persistence, download or other suspicious follow-up activity. One caveat: the alert comes from Splunk attack_data (T1489 service stop/disable), so this may be a scripted test rather than a real admin. The activity looks the same either way, and I'm judging it benign on the evidence in the graph.",
